WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.380
How did "The Wedding Singer" evolve from&nbsp;
just another slapstick comedy into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.280
beloved blockbuster hit? Here's what went&nbsp;
into making Adam Sandler's retro rom-com.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.660
According to "Wedding Singer" screenwriter&nbsp;
Tim Herlihy, there wasn't a conscious effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.840
to make the film drastically different&nbsp;
from "Billy Madison" or "Happy Gilmore."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.500
The formula had been working for both&nbsp;
the fans and the box office receipts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.760
so there wasn't a need to reinvent it. But as&nbsp;
work got underway on the pair's third film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.960
it ended up naturally evolving into a more&nbsp;
grounded and more romance-focused film.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.260
Herlihy credits the fact that he had&nbsp;
recently gotten married as part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.100
reason why the script began to become more of&nbsp;
a grown-up romantic comedy rather than another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.900
silly romp about a bumbling man-child. He also&nbsp;
says that the change was inspired by Sandler's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.500
terrific chemistry with Drew Barrymore,&nbsp;
and the fact that test audiences didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.920
mind scenes where her character was the&nbsp;
focus and Sandler wasn't on screen at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.360
The latter was deemed unacceptable by test&nbsp;
audiences of the previous two Sandler films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.705
forcing those movies to cut almost&nbsp;
any scene that Sandler wasn't in.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.100
Adam Sandler wisely surrounds himself with&nbsp;
fantastic character actors in his films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.600
and one of the best that he ever worked with&nbsp;
was Alexis Arquette. While Arquette's Boy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.080
George-obsessed "Wedding Singer" bandmate&nbsp;
could have easily been extremely one-note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.520
and only useful for a single punchline, George&nbsp;
Stitzer proved to be an effective and ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.480
presence throughout the film and was responsible&nbsp;
for just as many warm moments as funny ones.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.140
It seems obvious enough that Arquette's&nbsp;
performance came from a place of affection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.400
for the singer, but it isn't always clear how&nbsp;
the person being parodied is going to take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.900
impression. However, during a concert with his&nbsp;
band Culture Club the day after Arquette passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.180
away in 2016, George paid tribute to Arquette,&nbsp;
saying she played him, quote, "very hilariously"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.320
and that he enjoyed Arquette's work in the film.&nbsp;
He then dedicated his performance of his classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.240
song "Do You Really Want To Hurt Me" to Arquette,&nbsp;
as that was the song she got stuck singing over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.900
and over again to an increasingly frustrated&nbsp;
audience in her biggest scene in the film.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.300
"You suuuuuuck!"

00:01:48.418 --> 00:01:50.638
If you need a character to&nbsp;
have a real woman's voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.638 --> 00:01:55.020
you often need to get a woman to write her&nbsp;
words. That is exactly what happened when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.860
none other than Carrie Fisher was brought&nbsp;
in to do uncredited script doctor work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.520
"The Wedding Singer," specifically to tweak&nbsp;
the dialogue of Drew Barrymore's character.

00:02:03.188 --> 00:02:04.740
"I don't need a big wedding."

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.700
In fact, Fisher had been doing that very&nbsp;
thing for years, and she's worked on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.300
number of different movies. Her earliest&nbsp;
examples of uncredited dialogue work came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.680
in the original "Star Wars" films. She grew&nbsp;
frustrated when Harrison Ford's rewriting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.460
his own Han Solo dialogue began to have a&nbsp;
negative impact on Princess Leia's lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:26.040
so she decided to rewrite hers as well. George&nbsp;
Lucas liked her work so much that he asked her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.880
to write an episode of "The Young Indiana&nbsp;
Jones Chronicles" and also help him with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.960
the dialogue in the "Star Wars" prequels. Fisher&nbsp;
eventually became a sought after script doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:39.108
doing script work on such films as "Last Action&nbsp;
Hero," "Hook," "Scream 3," and "Coyote Ugly."

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.200
Adam Sandler movies, at least the ones&nbsp;
that he either co-wrote or were written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.400
specifically for him, have rarely been a&nbsp;
hit with the critics. It's only when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.820
appears in a movie that he merely acts&nbsp;
in — like "Punch-Drunk Love" or "Uncut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.020
Gems" — that critics manage to appreciate his&nbsp;
work. Considering he has a net worth of almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.860
half a billion dollars and was eventually&nbsp;
deemed worthy of $20 million-per-movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.000
paydays that are typically reserved for&nbsp;
blockbuster action stars, it's clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.560
the moviegoing public doesn't see eye to eye with&nbsp;
the critics. He's probably just fine with that.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.800
With all that being said, not all&nbsp;
Adam Sandler movies have been met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.620
with unanimous disapproval from critics. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.120
even "Happy Gilmore" managed to just squeak&nbsp;
by with a "Fresh" rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.580
But "The Wedding Singer" comes in at a respectable&nbsp;
69%, making it the highest-rated of films he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.720
penned, and fifth overall of his movies&nbsp;
to date. While a few of the higher-rated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.980
movies have comedic elements, they are&nbsp;
just as equally dramas, and the case can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.060
easily be made that "The Wedding Singer" is the&nbsp;
highest-rated pure comedy of Sandler's career.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.700
"Is it your confidence? Are&nbsp;
you nervous about your penis?"

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.080
Adam Sandler movies were basically an&nbsp;
annual event in the '90s. Every fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.340
meant a new Adam Sandler comedy — until New&nbsp;
Line Cinema messed that up when they decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.580
that "The Wedding Singer" would work better&nbsp;
if it was released close to Valentine's Day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:59.220
So they pushed it from its originally-planned&nbsp;
fall 1997 release into February 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.580
in hopes that couples would see it as part&nbsp;
of a romantic dinner and a movie date.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.140
It's hard to tell how much more business the new&nbsp;
release date actually drove to the movie and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.700
much of it was just Sandler's huge star power at&nbsp;
that time, but the box office tallies no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.400
made New Line very happy. Still, the side effect&nbsp;
was that 1997 saw zero new Adam Sandler movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:23.460
while 1998 saw two: "The Wedding Singer" in&nbsp;
February, followed by "The Waterboy" in November.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.740
Moviegoers clearly didn't have Sandler fatigue at&nbsp;
that point, as "The Waterboy" was also a huge hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:33.300
— much bigger than "The Wedding Singer" in fact,&nbsp;
with more than double its domestic box office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:39.360
Combined, Adam Sandler movies brought in roughly&nbsp;
$242 million at the box office that year just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.980
in the U.S.. With only two comedies, that's an&nbsp;
impressive figure no matter how it's distributed.

00:04:44.077 --> 00:04:47.557
"Is it true that you're in the&nbsp;
middle of a nervous breakdown?"

00:04:47.637 --> 00:04:48.057
"No!"

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.460
It's impossible to deny what a wonderful onscreen&nbsp;
couple Adam Sandler and Drew Barrymore make.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:57.660
Their chemistry is palpable from their very first&nbsp;
scene together in "The Wedding Singer," and it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.680
very easy to see how quickly they are able to&nbsp;
fall in love despite the obstacles that stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.580
in their way. Barrymore claims that she could&nbsp;
tell she and Sandler would be great together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.260
even before the two met. She says she approached&nbsp;
Sandler and revealed that she believed them to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.400
"cinematic soul mates," a claim which she admits&nbsp;
must have sounded crazy to him at the time.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.440
As crazy as it might have sounded, it proved&nbsp;
to be accurate. Not only were the two great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.060
together in "The Wedding Singer," but they&nbsp;
reteamed on "50 First Dates," which was yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.900
another example of how well they clicked&nbsp;
on-screen. It would also be the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.860
that Sandler revisited a female co-lead,&nbsp;
something he typically does only in direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.660
sequels like "Grown Ups 2." In fact, the duo&nbsp;
couldn't resist making a third movie together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.340
which they did in 2014's "Blended." That one&nbsp;
might have failed to recapture the magic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:45.134
their previous pairings, but it definitely&nbsp;
speaks to how well the two actors get along.

00:05:47.218 --> 00:05:49.355
"I think I'm in love with her."

00:05:49.777 --> 00:05:52.897
With so many scenes in "The Wedding&nbsp;
Singer" taking place at weddings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.897 --> 00:05:57.360
the movie is full of crowds of people that are&nbsp;
difficult to keep track of. Among those crowds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.720
is an actor who was at the time mostly&nbsp;
known only to stand-up comedy nerds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.420
but would soon be familiar to&nbsp;
mainstream audiences as well.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:07.440
During Robbie's bitter performance of "Love&nbsp;
Stinks," he goes around and gets various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.580
eclectic members of the crowd involved — from&nbsp;
a woman with sideburns to a man stuffing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:17.160
face to an entire table of people he regrettably&nbsp;
refers to as "mutants." Among the group of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.380
at table nine that Robbie is cruel to is comedian&nbsp;
Brian Posehn in his first film role. He's easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:27.300
miss at first, but once the entire table belts out&nbsp;
"love stinks!" in unison he's much easier to spot.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.140
Prior to "The Wedding Singer," Posehn&nbsp;
previously appeared in "Mr. Show with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:34.740
Bob and David," and he also landed&nbsp;
cameos in "Friends" and "NewsRadio."

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:38.280
After "The Wedding Singer," he scored&nbsp;
a recurring role on "Just Shoot Me!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.328
and a steady stream of TV and&nbsp;
movie appearances after that.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:45.720
Billy Idol's appearance in "The Wedding Singer"&nbsp;
likely was a surprise to audiences at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:49.620
but it was a pleasant one. Idol&nbsp;
showed up as himself, a rock star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.260
flying first class who would&nbsp;
end up helping Robbie win Julia.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:56.520
Idol would later admit that the main reason he did&nbsp;
the film was that his son was a huge Sandler fan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:00.360
and he reasoned that if he was going to have&nbsp;
to see the movie anyway, he might as well be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:04.980
in it. He showed up during the pivotal scene where&nbsp;
Julia finally officially picks Robbie over Glen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.120
and it ended up being good for his career.&nbsp;
The cameo introduced him to a whole new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.080
generation of fans who Idol says continued&nbsp;
to follow him for years after the fact.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.440
"Sir? Is that Billy Idol?"

00:07:18.434 --> 00:07:20.340
"I believe it is."

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.420
Although Robbie sings a lot in "The Wedding&nbsp;
Singer," both as part of his gig and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:28.680
in impromptu performances for Julia,&nbsp;
it isn't technically a musical. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.740
became one when screenwriter Tim Herlihy&nbsp;
teamed with Chad Beguelin and Matthew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.820
Sklar to adapt it into a full-fledged&nbsp;
Broadway production. After previews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:40.260
it officially opened in February 2006 and&nbsp;
ran until the end of the year on Broadway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:45.420
before becoming a touring production in 2007 and&nbsp;
beyond. Versions of the "Wedding Singer" musical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:49.980
have been performed in at least 30 cities in the&nbsp;
U.S., as well as Canada, Europe, and Australia.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:54.240
While "The Wedding Singer" movie was never&nbsp;
going to rack up any Academy Award nominations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.380
the musical was much better respected by&nbsp;
the live theater counterpart. The show was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.540
nominated for five Tony Awards: Best Musical,&nbsp;
Best Book of a Musical, Best Original Score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.620
Best Choreography, and Best Performance of&nbsp;
an Actor In A Leading Role in a Musical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.400
It didn't end up winning any of those&nbsp;
awards, but it's still impressive that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.041
the "Wedding Singer" musical can proclaim&nbsp;
that it's a five-time Tony Award nominee.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.060
These days, we take for granted&nbsp;
the ability to stream music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.720
but it wasn't that way in the '90s. In the case&nbsp;
of "The Wedding Singer," buying the soundtrack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:25.980
was an easy and affordable way to instantly own&nbsp;
a collection of a dozen or so classic '80s hits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:30.060
all in one place. A lot of people jumped at&nbsp;
that chance, as the soundtrack went double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:34.260
platinum in the U.S. and also sold very&nbsp;
well in Canada, Australia, and the U.K.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:38.640
The soundtrack for "The Wedding Singer" was so&nbsp;
successful, in fact, that it was the rare example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.200
of a movie soundtrack later having a "Volume 2."&nbsp;
The second installment included songs from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.340
movie that weren't among the first batch, like&nbsp;
Adam Sandler's "Grow Old With You." In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:51.060
both "Wedding Singer" soundtracks are so&nbsp;
well loved that they've even since been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.643
re-released in vinyl format, which is&nbsp;
fitting for a collection of '80s tunes.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.140
As Adam Sandler built up his&nbsp;
resume of lead roles in the '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:01.560
there was a time when each movie did&nbsp;
better than the last at the box office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.160
"Billy Madison" grossed $25 million,&nbsp;
"Happy Gilmore" topped that at $39 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.780
and then "The Wedding Singer" blew them both&nbsp;
out of the water with an $80 million domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:17.834
box office tally. Needless to say, Sandler&nbsp;
had arrived as a bankable comedy movie lead.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:22.020
"Sir, one more outburst I will strangle you&nbsp;
with my microphone wire, you understand me?"

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.920
But what "The Wedding Singer" also announced&nbsp;
was the arrival of Adam Sandler as a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.120
superstar. While his previous two films&nbsp;
barely did any business outside of the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:35.280
"The Wedding Singer" added a combined $43 million&nbsp;
in its global release, pushing him over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:40.380
$100 million mark worldwide for the first time.&nbsp;
Granted, he had no trouble passing $100 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:45.300
just in the U.S. on his next handful or so of&nbsp;
releases, not counting notable early outlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.440
dud "Little Nicky." But it's still important to&nbsp;
take note of the movie that first got him into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.434
nine figure club and extended his reach beyond the&nbsp;
States, and that movie was "The Wedding Singer."

00:09:54.434 --> 00:09:56.400
Adam Sandler movies reuse a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.720
same actors, but he's also been generous when it&nbsp;
comes to finding parts for some of his old pals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.820
including Allen Covert, Peter Dante , and&nbsp;
Jonathan Loughran, giving them roles both in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:09.240
front of and behind the camera. Loughran&nbsp;
has also served as Sandler's assistant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:13.320
while Covert has been a producer on basically&nbsp;
every Happy Madison production to date.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:18.120
Early in his career, Sandler initially didn't have&nbsp;
the clout for this, but on "The Wedding Singer,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.620
Sandler could finally pull the strings needed to&nbsp;
not only get Covert a major part as Robbie's best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:26.880
friend, but he also brought Peter Dante into the&nbsp;
fold with a minor role in the beginning. Covert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.060
Dante, and Loughran would go on to have&nbsp;
bigger roles in subsequent Sandler movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.180
and have even forged careers&nbsp;
outside of Happy Madison as well. 
 

